 "article": {
  "title": "Why Handwriting Still Matters in a Digital Age",
  "topic": "教育·学习方式",
  "level": "CET-4",
  "words": 219,
  "paras": [
   "In an age when nearly everything can be typed, tapped or spoken into a device, the simple act of writing by hand can feel old-fashioned. Yet a growing body of research suggests that putting pen to paper still helps us learn, remember and think.",
   "One reason lies in how the brain works. Typing is fast: words appear almost as quickly as we think them. Writing is slower, so the writer must decide what matters. This forced delay gives the brain time to process ideas more deeply. Studies of students have found that those who take notes by hand usually remember more of the key points than those who type.",
   "Handwriting also leaves a visible record of our thinking. Crossing out a word, drawing an arrow, or squeezing a note into the margin shows how an idea developed. These small marks are hard to reproduce on a screen, yet they can help us review and reflect.",
   "Does this mean we should abandon our keyboards? Of course not. Typing is faster for long documents and easier to share. The wiser approach is to choose the right tool for the job: type when speed matters, but write by hand when the goal is to understand and remember. In a world of screens, a pen is still a powerful learning tool."
  ],
  "zh": "在一切都能敲进设备的时代，手写仍有助于学习与记忆。原因在于：打字太快，大脑来不及消化；手写较慢，逼人先判断重点，从而理解更深，记笔记的学生也往往记住更多要点。手写还能留下思考的痕迹——涂改、箭头、页边批注，都记录了想法的发展过程。文章并非主张放弃键盘，而是建议：追求速度时用键盘，追求理解和记忆时用手写。",
